\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


A witness of decodability must be a finite object that verifies the claimed decoding property of the specified code. This may concern a Goppa presentation, decoding a particular received word, or a guaranteed decoding radius. A witness of nondecodability would have to certify failure of the corresponding property.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
What witnesses are there of Goppa decodability or non-decodability? \href{http://aimpl.org/quantumalg/1/}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Scope qualification.}
The source does not distinguish code recognition from received-word decoding or specify a radius; these different tasks are kept explicit rather than conflated.
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record partially solved. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness.

